        "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln\u2061(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=${{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "First note that $ln\u2061(0.9)<0.$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{4}$ looks like an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $ln\u2061(0.9)$ causes $y=ln\u2061(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln\u2061(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{4})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
